---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Container from '../components/astro/Container.astro';
import SectionHero from '../components/astro/SectionHero.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Summary">
  <section class="border-b border-ink-100">
    <Container width="wide" class="py-16 md:py-24">
      <SectionHero
        as="h1"
        eyebrow="Part 07 · Summary & closing thoughts"
        title="What the work demonstrated, what surprised it, and what it should not overclaim."
        lede="The most useful learnings were not generic benchmark wins. They were design discoveries: where governance had to become structural, where evaluation had to become behavioral, and where the real failure causes actually lived."
      />
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section>
    <Container width="wide" class="py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-14 text-ink-800">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">The paradox</p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
            Enterprises have invested heavily in AI and seen comparatively little operational
            impact. The bottleneck is not model capability — it is the
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900"
              >governed translation from enterprise intent into context an agent can actually act
              on.</strong
            >
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">Why most existing answers fall short</p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
            Platform AI products tend to treat enterprises as interchangeable. They are not.
            Security models, authority hierarchies, internal systems, and compliance obligations
            are specific to the organization, and a generic context engine flattens differences
            that matter. Most of the 2026 context-engine boom has focused on retrieval over code;
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900"
              >far fewer products are solving governed translation from enterprise intent into
              scoped, authority-aware agent context.</strong
            >
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent-soft/40 py-6 pl-7 pr-6">
          <p class="eyebrow mb-4 text-accent">My argument</p>
          <blockquote
            class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold leading-snug tracking-tight text-ink-900 md:text-3xl"
          >
            A hand-built orchestration layer is not architectural ambition. It is a forcing
            function — the path that makes an enterprise
            <span class="text-accent"
              >confront its own security, reliability, and business constraints</span
            > rather than inherit someone else&rsquo;s answers.
          </blockquote>
        </figure>

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">The approach in three notes</p>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">Spec-driven before runtime.</strong> A
              four-document stack (PRD → Design Doc → Context Contract → Agent Specs) governs what
              context can be assembled, who can see it, and what each agent must produce. The
              behavior of the system is decided in writing before any code runs.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">Deterministic supervisor at runtime.</strong>
              A Python state machine owns step order, retrieval routing, and the conditions under
              which the pipeline advances, escalates, or halts. The supervisor never invents — it
              preserves.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">Governed agentic retrieval.</strong>
              Per-source lanes — dense, lexical, and direct structured access — feed scoped bundles
              assembled by the supervisor. Authority tiers and permissions are honored before the
              agent ever sees anything.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">What the demo showed</p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
            Four recorded runs of a vendor-onboarding pipeline. Three terminal status emissions
            surfaced exactly when they should:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span
                class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center rounded-full border border-spruce-700/30 bg-spruce-50 px-2.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.62rem] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-spruce-700"
              >
                Complete
              </span>
              <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
                when evidence resolves the question and the pipeline can proceed.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span
                class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center rounded-full border border-accent/40 bg-accent-soft/60 px-2.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.62rem] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-accent"
              >
                Escalated
              </span>
              <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
                when evidence is conflicting or the decision is ambiguous, and a human owner must
                decide.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span
                class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center rounded-full border border-rose-700/30 bg-rose-50 px-2.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.62rem] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-rose-700"
              >
                Blocked
              </span>
              <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
                when required evidence is absent, and the run cannot honestly continue.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed">
            These are not error codes. They are
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">governed signals</strong> — the system telling
            its operators what is true, what is in question, and what is missing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">Closing</p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
            Enterprise AI does not become trustworthy by adding more context. It becomes
            trustworthy when the
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-900">context layer is built deliberately</strong>,
            the orchestration is inspectable, and the system&rsquo;s answers — including its
            refusals — are routable to the people who own them. That, more than anything else, is
            what this project set out to demonstrate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
